+# ambed software vocoder (SoftAMBE)
+
+ambed can transcode D-Star (AMBE 3600x2400) to and from DMR/YSF (AMBE+2
+3600x2450) in software, without a DVSI USB-3000/3003/3012 device.
+
+## How it works
+
+`CSoftAmbeInterface` behaves like a DVSI device as far as the rest of ambed
+is concerned: it exposes vocoder channels, decodes AMBE to PCM, runs the
+normal signal processing (AGC/gain) and encodes PCM back to AMBE.
+
+The vocoder itself lives in `swvocoder/` and is the open source software
+vocoder from [DroidStar](https://github.com/nostar/DroidStar) by Doug McLain
+AD8DP:
+
+- `swvocoder/mbe/`: mbelib decoder and an AMBE / AMBE+2 encoder derived
+  from OP25 (ISC licence).
+- `swvocoder/imbe_vocoder/`: Pavel Yazev's fixed-point IMBE analysis,
+  used by the encoder for pitch and voicing estimation (GPL).
+
+No firmware or proprietary code is used.
+
+## Build
+
+```bash
+cd ambed
+make NO_FTDI=1      # software only, no libftd2xx needed
+# or
+make                # hardware + software, needs libftd2xx as before
+sudo make install
+```
+
+## Run
+
+The number of software transcoding pairs comes from the `AMBED_SOFT_PAIRS`
+environment variable. Each pair handles one D-Star to DMR stream and one
+DMR to D-Star stream at the same time. Unset or 0 disables it.
+
+```bash
+sudo AMBED_SOFT_PAIRS=4 /ambed/ambed 127.0.0.1
+```
+
+When DVSI hardware is also present, hardware channels are used first and
+software channels only when they are all busy.
+
+## Tuning
+
+All audio settings are read at startup from environment variables. With
+the XLX-SoftAMBE installer they live in `/ambed/ambed.conf`, loaded by
+`ambed.service` through `EnvironmentFile=`, each with a description. The
+startup log (`journalctl -u ambed | grep SoftAMBE`) prints the values in
+use. Whole numbers only; out-of-range values are clamped and invalid ones
+fall back to the default.
+
+| Variable | Default | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `AMBED_SOFT_PAIRS` | 0 | transcoding pairs (0 = software vocoder off) |
+| `AMBED_SOFT_GAIN_DSTAR_TO_DMR` | -20 | output level, D-Star -> DMR/YSF (dB, +/-24) |
+| `AMBED_SOFT_EQ_DSTAR_TO_DMR` | 0 | presence boost, D-Star -> DMR/YSF (dB, 0-12, 0 = EQ off) |
+| `AMBED_SOFT_GATE_DSTAR_TO_DMR` | 0 | expander threshold, D-Star -> DMR/YSF (dBFS, 0 = off) |
+| `AMBED_SOFT_GAIN_DMR_TO_DSTAR` | 17 | output level, DMR/YSF -> D-Star (dB, +/-24) |
+| `AMBED_SOFT_EQ_DMR_TO_DSTAR` | 0 | presence boost, DMR/YSF -> D-Star (dB, 0-12, 0 = EQ off) |
+| `AMBED_SOFT_GATE_DMR_TO_DSTAR` | -45 | expander threshold, DMR/YSF -> D-Star (dBFS, 0 = off) |
+| `AMBED_SOFT_EQ_LOWCUT_HZ` | 250 | equalizer high-pass (Hz, 0-600, 0 = none) |
+| `AMBED_SOFT_EQ_PEAK_HZ` | 1800 | equalizer peak centre (Hz, 500-3500) |
+| `AMBED_SOFT_GATE_RATIO` | 2 | expander dB of cut per dB under threshold (1-10) |
+| `AMBED_SOFT_GATE_MAX_DB` | 20 | largest expander cut (dB, 0-40) |
+| `AMBED_SOFT_GATE_HOLD_MS` | 100 | expander hold after speech (ms, 0-1000) |
+| `AMBED_SOFT_GATE_RELEASE_MS` | 270 | expander time to close fully (ms, 20-2000) |
+| `AMBED_SOFT_IDLE_RESET_MS` | 500 | vocoder reset after this much silence (ms, 100-5000) |
+
+The defaults (also in `csoftambeinterface.h`) come from on-air tests with
+a Yaesu FT-1DR and Icom radios.
+
+### Why the gains are large
+
+The software decoders are level-matched to each other, but radios decode
+this encoder's AMBE+2 much louder and its AMBE much quieter (the DVSI
+`CODECGAIN_*` values compensate the same way with -10/+10 dB).
+
+The gain is applied to the encoder's quantized gain parameter, not to the
+PCM. Attenuating the PCM by 20 dB pushes quiet passages under the IMBE
+analysis floor (harmonic amplitudes are clamped to 1), which is heard as a
+constant hiss in pauses; boosting it by 17 dB clips the peaks. Measured
+speech-to-pause ratio on a test signal with -60 dBFS background noise:
+
+| Path | PCM gain | Encoder gain |
+|---|---|---|
+| D-Star -> DMR, -20 dB | 23.7 dB | 33.5 dB |
+| DMR -> D-Star, +17 dB | 28.8 dB | 32.9 dB |
+
+### Equalizer and expander
+
+Radios decode this encoder's AMBE with too much energy under 400 Hz and
+too little at 0.8-2.5 kHz, which sounds boomy and muffled; the software
+decoders do not show it, so it was measured from on-air recordings. Before
+the encoder of a path, ambed can apply:
+
+- an equalizer: high-pass at `EQ_LOWCUT_HZ` plus a broad peak at
+  `EQ_PEAK_HZ` (Q 0.6), off by default (on-air tests preferred the plain
+  DMR/YSF -> D-Star sound once the gain moved into the encoder);
+- a downward expander: frames under the threshold are attenuated
+  `GATE_RATIO` dB per dB below it, up to `GATE_MAX_DB`, on the encoder
+  gain parameter. It opens at once, holds `GATE_HOLD_MS`, then closes over
+  `GATE_RELEASE_MS`, so word endings are kept.
+
+On a test signal with -50 dBFS room noise, the expander lowers the noise
+in pauses by about 10 dB (speech-to-pause 28.3 -> 36.7 dB with EQ 9 dB)
+without changing the speech level.
+
+## Measured (x86_64 Xeon 2.8 GHz)
+
+- Round-trip time per frame: about 3 ms (a DVSI stick is 40-100 ms).
+- CPU: about 8% of one core per active stream, including ambed overhead.
+
+## Patents
+
+AMBE and AMBE+2 are DVSI technologies. Check the patent situation in your
+country before using this.
